  "payload": {
    "question": "Why does chunk boundary placement matter, and how does recursive splitting help?",
    "model": "claude-opus-4-8",
    "answer": [
      {
        "text": "A retriever only ever sees chunks, so it can return nothing that a chunk does not contain",
        "citations": [
          1
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": ". Where a boundary falls is therefore a recall decision",
        "citations": [
          3
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": ". ",
        "citations": []
      },
      {
        "text": "Fixed windows are the cheap baseline, cutting every N tokens",
        "citations": [
          4
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": ", and overlap between windows protects answers that straddle a cut",
        "citations": [
          5
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": ". ",
        "citations": []
      },
      {
        "text": "Recursive splitting helps because it moves to a finer separator only when a piece is still over the token budget",
        "citations": [
          2
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": ", so it keeps paragraphs and sentences whole wherever it can, which is why most frameworks use it by default.",
        "citations": []
      }
    ],
    "citations": [
